###  https://w3id.org/tvbo/AlgorithmCompositionMode#combined
<https://w3id.org/tvbo/AlgorithmCompositionMode#combined> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                          rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/tvbo/AlgorithmCompositionMode> ;
                                                          rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/tvbo/struct> ;
                                                          rdfs:label "combined (AlgorithmCompositionMode)" ;
                                                          <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The included algorithm's update rules are merged into the outer loop and applied ONCE per outer iteration (1:1). Use when both algorithms update at the same cadence on the same observations. This is the default." ;
                                                          <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "combined" .


###  https://w3id.org/tvbo/AlgorithmCompositionMode#nested
<https://w3id.org/tvbo/AlgorithmCompositionMode#nested> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                        rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/tvbo/AlgorithmCompositionMode> ;
                                                        rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/tvbo/struct> ;
                                                        rdfs:label "nested (AlgorithmCompositionMode)" ;
                                                        <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The included algorithm runs as a full inner loop on EACH outer iteration, re-converging before the outer update rules are applied. Use when the inner algorithm maintains an invariant the outer one would otherwise perturb — e.g. FIC holding the E-I working point (mean S_e = 0.25) while EIB retunes per-edge coupling. The outer update's validity depends on that invariant, so the inner loop must re-settle it between every outer step." ;
                                                        <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "nested" .


